1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,123
Just after midnight on January 24, 1961, a

2
00:00:04,143 --> 00:00:07,328
B-52 bomber came apart in the sky over

3
00:00:07,348 --> 00:00:08,593
North Carolina.

4
00:00:09,163 --> 00:00:11,268
It was carrying two hydrogen bombs.

5
00:00:11,838 --> 00:00:13,935
One came down under its parachute and was

6
00:00:13,955 --> 00:00:15,175
found hanging in a tree.

7
00:00:15,645 --> 00:00:17,833
The other hit a waterlogged field so hard

8
00:00:17,853 --> 00:00:19,771
that part of it was never recovered.

9
00:00:20,341 --> 00:00:22,409
Twenty years later, the Pentagon's own

10
00:00:22,429 --> 00:00:24,662
summary of the accident needed just three

11
00:00:24,682 --> 00:00:25,870
words for the outcome.

12
00:00:26,453 --> 00:00:27,928
No explosion occurred.

13
00:00:28,498 --> 00:00:31,154
But in 1969, a safety engineer at Sandia,

14
00:00:31,174 --> 00:00:33,764
one of the laboratories behind America's

15
00:00:33,784 --> 00:00:36,309
nuclear weapons, wrote a very different

16
00:00:36,329 --> 00:00:36,896
sentence.

17
00:00:37,467 --> 00:00:40,123
One simple, dynamo-technology, low voltage

18
00:00:40,143 --> 00:00:42,799
switch stood between the United States and

19
00:00:42,819 --> 00:00:44,073
a major catastrophe.

20
00:00:44,643 --> 00:00:46,540
This is the file on Goldsboro.

21
00:00:47,010 --> 00:00:47,990
Let's open it.

22
00:00:52,243 --> 00:00:54,190
To understand that night, start with the

23
00:00:54,210 --> 00:00:55,468
job that bomber was doing.

24
00:00:56,088 --> 00:00:58,569
In those years, Strategic Air Command kept

25
00:00:58,589 --> 00:01:00,891
some of its B-52s in the air around the

26
00:01:00,911 --> 00:01:02,796
clock, carrying nuclear weapons.

27
00:01:03,266 --> 00:01:05,365
The Pentagon called it airborne alert.

28
00:01:05,935 --> 00:01:08,193
Years later, the Pentagon published

29
00:01:08,213 --> 00:01:10,796
summaries of 32 accidents involving U.S.

30
00:01:10,816 --> 00:01:11,837
nuclear weapons.

31
00:01:12,307 --> 00:01:14,482
Most of the aircraft accidents on that

32
00:01:14,502 --> 00:01:16,619
list happened on ferry flights, or on

33
00:01:16,639 --> 00:01:18,699
Strategic Air Command airborne alert

34
00:01:18,719 --> 00:01:19,218
missions.

35
00:01:19,788 --> 00:01:21,938
That meant real weapons, aloft day and

36
00:01:21,958 --> 00:01:23,765
night, often over American soil.

37
00:01:24,235 --> 00:01:26,201
And it meant that sooner or later, one of

38
00:01:26,221 --> 00:01:27,412
those planes would crash.

39
00:01:27,982 --> 00:01:30,039
The question was what a crash would do to

40
00:01:30,059 --> 00:01:30,546
the bombs.

41
00:01:31,116 --> 00:01:33,576
In 1958, documents from the Joint Chiefs

42
00:01:33,596 --> 00:01:36,118
of Staff called the risk of an accidental

43
00:01:36,138 --> 00:01:38,722
nuclear detonation of the newer sealed-pit

44
00:01:38,742 --> 00:01:40,458
weapons a negligible factor.

45
00:01:41,028 --> 00:01:43,384
That same year, a mishap at Sidi Slimane,

46
00:01:43,404 --> 00:01:45,760
in Morocco, raised a new worry inside the

47
00:01:45,780 --> 00:01:46,397
government.

48
00:01:46,867 --> 00:01:48,956
What would happen as Western Europe filled

49
00:01:48,976 --> 00:01:50,764
up with American nuclear stockpiles?

50
00:01:51,334 --> 00:01:54,045
In February 1959, a Strategic Air Command

51
00:01:54,065 --> 00:01:56,443
letter described a point of balance,

52
00:01:56,463 --> 00:01:58,508
between safety on one side, and

53
00:01:58,528 --> 00:02:00,906
reliability and reaction time on the

54
00:02:00,926 --> 00:02:01,306
other.

55
00:02:01,876 --> 00:02:04,012
That same month, a Sandia study put it

56
00:02:04,032 --> 00:02:04,466
bluntly.

57
00:02:04,936 --> 00:02:06,913
Safety, it said, was fundamentally a

58
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:08,577
matter of playing percentages.

59
00:02:09,147 --> 00:02:11,765
Its estimate for the risk of a spontaneous

60
00:02:11,785 --> 00:02:14,152
nuclear detonation, not counting human

61
00:02:14,172 --> 00:02:16,036
error, was one in 100 million.

62
00:02:16,956 --> 00:02:19,264
Then came January 1961.

63
00:02:19,884 --> 00:02:22,450
Late on January 23, a B-52G based at

64
00:02:22,470 --> 00:02:25,036
Seymour Johnson Air Force Base, near

65
00:02:25,056 --> 00:02:27,981
Goldsboro, North Carolina, was flying one

66
00:02:28,001 --> 00:02:29,274
of those missions.

67
00:02:29,614 --> 00:02:31,843
It was three days after John F. Kennedy

68
00:02:31,863 --> 00:02:32,535
took office.

69
00:02:33,005 --> 00:02:35,264
According to the National Archives, its

70
00:02:35,284 --> 00:02:36,550
call sign was Keep 19.

71
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:38,324
Eight men were aboard.

72
00:02:38,664 --> 00:02:40,753
The aircraft commander was Major Walter

73
00:02:40,773 --> 00:02:41,185
Tulloch.

74
00:02:41,755 --> 00:02:43,778
During in-flight refueling, the tanker's

75
00:02:43,798 --> 00:02:44,953
crew spotted a problem.

76
00:02:45,423 --> 00:02:47,505
Fuel was leaking from the bomber's right

77
00:02:47,525 --> 00:02:47,768
wing.

78
00:02:48,338 --> 00:02:50,893
According to the National Archives, the

79
00:02:50,913 --> 00:02:53,403
bomber lost more than 5,400 gallons of

80
00:02:53,423 --> 00:02:55,978
fuel, about 37,000 pounds, in less than

81
00:02:55,998 --> 00:02:56,902
three minutes.

82
00:02:57,472 --> 00:02:59,635
It was ordered to Seymour Johnson for an

83
00:02:59,655 --> 00:03:00,617
emergency landing.

84
00:03:01,187 --> 00:03:02,385
It never got there.

85
00:03:02,968 --> 00:03:05,449
The right wing failed, and the aircraft

86
00:03:05,469 --> 00:03:08,015
broke apart, somewhere between 2,000 and

87
00:03:08,035 --> 00:03:08,785
10,000 feet.

88
00:03:09,355 --> 00:03:11,474
Major Tulloch ordered the crew to bail

89
00:03:11,494 --> 00:03:11,700
out.

90
00:03:12,170 --> 00:03:14,158
According to the National Archives, one

91
00:03:14,178 --> 00:03:16,115
man climbed out through the top hatch.

92
00:03:16,685 --> 00:03:18,107
Five of the eight survived.

93
00:03:18,677 --> 00:03:19,657
Three did not.

94
00:03:20,077 --> 00:03:22,565
The National Archives names them as Major

95
00:03:22,585 --> 00:03:24,829
Shelton, Major Richards, and Sergeant

96
00:03:24,849 --> 00:03:25,318
Barnish.

97
00:03:25,888 --> 00:03:28,064
In military language, an accident like

98
00:03:28,084 --> 00:03:29,278
this has a code name.

99
00:03:29,861 --> 00:03:31,017
A Broken Arrow.

100
00:03:31,586 --> 00:03:33,931
The wreckage was scattered across roughly

101
00:03:33,951 --> 00:03:36,066
two square miles of farmland near the

102
00:03:36,086 --> 00:03:38,027
community of Faro, about ten miles

103
00:03:38,047 --> 00:03:40,219
northeast of Seymour Johnson Air Force

104
00:03:40,239 --> 00:03:40,507
Base.

105
00:03:41,427 --> 00:03:42,871
So what happened to the bombs?

106
00:03:43,491 --> 00:03:45,900
They were Mark 39 thermonuclear weapons.

107
00:03:46,370 --> 00:03:48,505
Each, according to the National Archives,

108
00:03:48,525 --> 00:03:50,502
had a yield of three to four megatons.

109
00:03:50,842 --> 00:03:53,523
Roughly 250 times the bomb dropped on

110
00:03:53,543 --> 00:03:54,253
Hiroshima.

111
00:03:54,823 --> 00:03:57,559
Each weighed at least 6,500 pounds, and

112
00:03:57,579 --> 00:03:59,184
was about 12 feet long.

113
00:03:59,654 --> 00:04:03,845
More than 700 were built, between 1957 and

114
00:04:03,865 --> 00:04:04,346
1959.

115
00:04:04,916 --> 00:04:06,787
As the bomber broke up, both of them

116
00:04:06,807 --> 00:04:08,258
separated from the aircraft.

117
00:04:08,828 --> 00:04:10,803
A month later, two Sandia engineers

118
00:04:10,823 --> 00:04:13,139
delivered a detailed analysis of what had

119
00:04:13,159 --> 00:04:14,336
happened inside them.

120
00:04:14,806 --> 00:04:16,841
Their report even got the town wrong.

121
00:04:17,181 --> 00:04:18,908
Its title says Greensboro.

122
00:04:19,478 --> 00:04:21,568
Both bombs, they found, had been set to

123
00:04:21,588 --> 00:04:21,839
safe.

124
00:04:22,309 --> 00:04:24,279
But the crash itself started the fuzing

125
00:04:24,299 --> 00:04:25,555
sequence on both of them.

126
00:04:26,125 --> 00:04:27,334
Take the first bomb.

127
00:04:27,804 --> 00:04:30,137
As the plane broke apart, lanyards pulled

128
00:04:30,157 --> 00:04:32,490
out its safing pins, and the crash yanked

129
00:04:32,510 --> 00:04:34,728
the pins on the generator that supplied

130
00:04:34,748 --> 00:04:35,818
its electric power.

131
00:04:36,388 --> 00:04:37,650
Its parachute opened.

132
00:04:37,990 --> 00:04:40,194
And according to later accounts, its timer

133
00:04:40,214 --> 00:04:40,405
ran.

134
00:04:40,975 --> 00:04:43,043
What remained was the Arm/Safe switch,

135
00:04:43,063 --> 00:04:44,307
controlled by the crew.

136
00:04:44,889 --> 00:04:46,343
It was still set to safe.

137
00:04:46,763 --> 00:04:48,770
And that, Sandia's analysts found,

138
00:04:48,790 --> 00:04:50,559
prevented a nuclear explosion.

139
00:04:51,129 --> 00:04:52,878
That bomb drifted down under its

140
00:04:52,898 --> 00:04:54,924
parachute, and was found hanging in a

141
00:04:54,944 --> 00:04:56,471
tree, essentially undamaged.

142
00:04:57,042 --> 00:04:59,227
The second bomb's parachute never opened.

143
00:04:59,697 --> 00:05:01,712
It fell free, hit the waterlogged ground

144
00:05:01,732 --> 00:05:03,290
at high speed, and broke apart.

145
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:06,071
Because its parachute never opened, its

146
00:05:06,091 --> 00:05:08,131
timer never started its high-voltage

147
00:05:08,151 --> 00:05:08,589
battery.

148
00:05:09,059 --> 00:05:11,254
But when its Arm/Safe switch was dug out

149
00:05:11,274 --> 00:05:13,303
of the mud, the indicator read armed.

150
00:05:13,872 --> 00:05:16,029
Sandia's analysts concluded that the

151
00:05:16,049 --> 00:05:18,567
impact had spun the indicator, and damaged

152
00:05:18,587 --> 00:05:19,897
the switch's contacts.

153
00:05:20,367 --> 00:05:22,433
That bomb, they found, was not close to

154
00:05:22,453 --> 00:05:22,968
going off.

155
00:05:23,538 --> 00:05:25,504
The close call was the bomb in the tree.

156
00:05:26,425 --> 00:05:27,559
Then came the digging.

157
00:05:28,179 --> 00:05:30,563
According to the National Archives, over

158
00:05:30,583 --> 00:05:33,027
eight days the recovery team dug out much

159
00:05:33,047 --> 00:05:35,191
of the buried bomb, including its 92

160
00:05:35,211 --> 00:05:35,852
detonators.

161
00:05:36,322 --> 00:05:39,024
Its nuclear pit came out on January 29.

162
00:05:39,594 --> 00:05:41,323
But the thermonuclear stage, the

163
00:05:41,343 --> 00:05:42,798
secondary, was never found.

164
00:05:43,368 --> 00:05:45,795
The Pentagon's summary says the excavation

165
00:05:45,815 --> 00:05:47,893
went down 50 feet in the waterlogged

166
00:05:47,913 --> 00:05:48,417
farmland.

167
00:05:48,887 --> 00:05:50,752
A portion of the weapon, containing

168
00:05:50,772 --> 00:05:52,475
uranium, could not be recovered.

169
00:05:53,045 --> 00:05:54,819
So the Air Force bought an easement.

170
00:05:55,159 --> 00:05:56,822
To dig there, you need permission.

171
00:05:57,391 --> 00:05:59,304
And the summary adds a final line.

172
00:05:59,774 --> 00:06:02,117
There is no detectable radiation, and no

173
00:06:02,137 --> 00:06:03,239
hazard in the area.

174
00:06:03,809 --> 00:06:05,622
There was also the question of what to

175
00:06:05,642 --> 00:06:06,394
tell the public.

176
00:06:06,864 --> 00:06:08,913
The Pentagon's policy, its summary

177
00:06:08,933 --> 00:06:11,346
explains, is normally to neither confirm

178
00:06:11,366 --> 00:06:13,780
nor deny the presence of nuclear weapons

179
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:15,118
at any specific place.

180
00:06:15,689 --> 00:06:18,133
After an accident, it says, their presence

181
00:06:18,153 --> 00:06:20,422
may or may not be divulged at the time,

182
00:06:20,442 --> 00:06:22,652
depending on the possibility of public

183
00:06:22,672 --> 00:06:23,591
hazard or alarm.

184
00:06:24,511 --> 00:06:25,912
So how close did it come?

185
00:06:26,532 --> 00:06:28,896
Sandia's analysis pointed to the Arm/Safe

186
00:06:28,916 --> 00:06:30,349
switch on the first bomb.

187
00:06:30,819 --> 00:06:33,180
In the National Security Archive's summary

188
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:35,504
of that report, the switch worked exactly

189
00:06:35,524 --> 00:06:37,659
as it was supposed to, and prevented a

190
00:06:37,679 --> 00:06:38,679
nuclear explosion.

191
00:06:39,249 --> 00:06:41,178
The analysts had a second concern.

192
00:06:41,518 --> 00:06:44,196
Those lanyards were already scheduled to

193
00:06:44,216 --> 00:06:46,827
be removed, under a modification called

194
00:06:46,847 --> 00:06:47,367
ALT 197.

195
00:06:47,837 --> 00:06:50,454
They urged that it be done quickly, across

196
00:06:50,474 --> 00:06:52,904
the whole Mark 15 and 39 family used on

197
00:06:52,924 --> 00:06:53,846
airborne alert.

198
00:06:54,416 --> 00:06:56,694
Defense Secretary Robert McNamara later

199
00:06:56,714 --> 00:06:58,580
described the accident this way.

200
00:06:59,149 --> 00:07:01,174
By the slightest margin of chance,

201
00:07:01,194 --> 00:07:03,400
literally the failure of two wires to

202
00:07:03,420 --> 00:07:05,745
cross, a nuclear explosion was averted.

203
00:07:06,315 --> 00:07:09,012
In 1969, Parker F. Jones, who supervised

204
00:07:09,032 --> 00:07:11,118
Sandia's nuclear weapons safety

205
00:07:11,138 --> 00:07:13,971
department, went back over the accident in

206
00:07:13,991 --> 00:07:16,484
a memo he called Goldsboro Revisited.

207
00:07:16,954 --> 00:07:18,750
He was responding to a book by the

208
00:07:18,770 --> 00:07:20,941
physicist Ralph Lapp, which had described

209
00:07:20,961 --> 00:07:21,475
the crash.

210
00:07:22,045 --> 00:07:23,953
His verdict on the weapon was blunt.

211
00:07:24,423 --> 00:07:26,864
The MK 39 Mod 2 bomb did not possess

212
00:07:26,884 --> 00:07:29,462
adequate safety for the airborne alert

213
00:07:29,482 --> 00:07:30,624
role in the B-52.

214
00:07:31,194 --> 00:07:33,667
That memo was declassified decades later.

215
00:07:34,137 --> 00:07:36,150
In 2013, the journalist Eric Schlosser,

216
00:07:36,170 --> 00:07:38,183
who had obtained it while researching a

217
00:07:38,203 --> 00:07:40,268
book, shared it with The Guardian, which

218
00:07:40,288 --> 00:07:40,946
published it.

219
00:07:41,516 --> 00:07:43,834
Not everyone reads the file the same way.

220
00:07:44,304 --> 00:07:46,587
Official statements, the National Archives

221
00:07:46,607 --> 00:07:48,726
notes, have said there was no danger of

222
00:07:48,746 --> 00:07:49,823
the bombs exploding.

223
00:07:50,293 --> 00:07:52,549
Others, it adds, indicate that most of the

224
00:07:52,569 --> 00:07:54,718
steps needed for a detonation did occur.

225
00:07:55,287 --> 00:07:58,028
And the Pentagon's 1981 summary says there

226
00:07:58,048 --> 00:08:00,722
has never been even a partial inadvertent

227
00:08:00,742 --> 00:08:02,299
U.S. nuclear detonation.

228
00:08:02,869 --> 00:08:03,955
Both can be true.

229
00:08:04,295 --> 00:08:05,734
The bomb did not go off.

230
00:08:06,204 --> 00:08:08,393
And according to Sandia's own analysts,

231
00:08:08,413 --> 00:08:10,602
one switch was the safeguard that held.

232
00:08:11,522 --> 00:08:13,686
Goldsboro was not the last accident.

233
00:08:14,306 --> 00:08:17,826
In January 1966, a B-52 collided with a

234
00:08:17,846 --> 00:08:20,459
tanker over Palomares, Spain.

235
00:08:20,799 --> 00:08:22,275
Seven crew members died.

236
00:08:22,745 --> 00:08:24,976
Conventional explosives in some of its

237
00:08:24,996 --> 00:08:27,346
weapons detonated, scattering plutonium.

238
00:08:27,686 --> 00:08:29,902
One bomb was lost at sea for nearly three

239
00:08:29,922 --> 00:08:31,593
months, until it was recovered.

240
00:08:32,163 --> 00:08:35,213
In January 1968, a B-52 crashed near

241
00:08:35,233 --> 00:08:36,663
Thule, Greenland.

242
00:08:37,133 --> 00:08:39,068
Its weapons broke up and scattered

243
00:08:39,088 --> 00:08:40,160
radioactive debris.

244
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:41,789
One crew member died.

245
00:08:42,359 --> 00:08:44,618
Those two, the Pentagon's summary says,

246
00:08:44,638 --> 00:08:47,072
were the only accidents that resulted in a

247
00:08:47,092 --> 00:08:49,525
widespread dispersal of nuclear materials.

248
00:08:50,096 --> 00:08:51,774
That year, airborne alert ended.

249
00:08:52,114 --> 00:08:54,187
The Pentagon later listed its reasons.

250
00:08:54,527 --> 00:08:57,217
The accidents, particularly Palomares and

251
00:08:57,237 --> 00:08:57,614
Thule.

252
00:08:57,954 --> 00:08:59,019
The rising cost.

253
00:08:59,359 --> 00:09:01,280
And a missile force that could take over

254
00:09:01,300 --> 00:09:02,493
part of the bombers' job.

255
00:09:03,063 --> 00:09:04,576
The standards tightened too.

256
00:09:04,916 --> 00:09:07,569
A policy review in the late 1960s set a

257
00:09:07,589 --> 00:09:07,912
goal.

258
00:09:08,252 --> 00:09:10,175
The risk of an accidental nuclear

259
00:09:10,195 --> 00:09:12,648
detonation should be no more than one in a

260
00:09:12,668 --> 00:09:15,121
million per year, for an arsenal of 10,000

261
00:09:15,141 --> 00:09:16,063
weapons or more.

262
00:09:16,633 --> 00:09:19,172
Even so, in 1987, a Sandia history of the

263
00:09:19,192 --> 00:09:21,669
safety program concluded that the United

264
00:09:21,689 --> 00:09:24,040
States could not claim, in an absolute

265
00:09:24,060 --> 00:09:26,225
sense, that it was doing everything

266
00:09:26,245 --> 00:09:28,784
possible to keep weapons safe, while they

267
00:09:28,804 --> 00:09:31,156
stayed fully assembled on combat-ready

268
00:09:31,176 --> 00:09:31,655
systems.

269
00:09:32,575 --> 00:09:34,358
Today, near the crash site, there is a

270
00:09:34,378 --> 00:09:34,595
sign.

271
00:09:35,215 --> 00:09:37,520
In 2012, North Carolina put up a state

272
00:09:37,540 --> 00:09:39,967
historical marker in Eureka, about three

273
00:09:39,987 --> 00:09:42,536
miles north of where the bomber came down.

274
00:09:43,106 --> 00:09:44,342
It reads, in full.

275
00:09:44,924 --> 00:09:48,004
B-52 transporting two nuclear bombs

276
00:09:48,024 --> 00:09:49,952
crashed, January 1961.

277
00:09:50,372 --> 00:09:52,157
Widespread disaster averted.

278
00:09:52,577 --> 00:09:54,677
